141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (This report presents the results of a Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing \(VAPT\) engagement conducted on the north.sevenkingdoms.local Active Directory environment. The objective of this assessment was to identify security weaknesses, evaluate their potential impact, and demonstrate how an attacker could exploit them to gain unauthorized access.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (The testing was performed from an internal attacker perspective, simulating a scenario where an attacker has network access but no initial credentials. The assessment focused on core Active Directory services, including authentication mechanisms \(Kerberos\), file sharing \(SMB\), and directory services \(LDAP\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (During the engagement, multiple vulnerabilities and misconfigurations were identified, particularly in the areas of credential management, access control, and delegation settings. These weaknesses were systematically exploited to demonstrate a real-world attack path, ultimately leading to full compromise of the Domain Controller.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
144 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (The findings in this report highlight critical gaps in security controls and emphasize the importance of implementing strong identity and access management practices, secure credential handling, and proper configuration of Active Directory features.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 93 0 R
/T (2.Executive Summary)
/E (2.Executive Summary)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (The Active Directory environment was assessed and found to be critically vulnerable to multiple high-impact security misconfigurations. During the engagement, it was possible to escalate privileges from an unauthenticated user to NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM on the Domain Controller, resulting in a complete compromise of the domain.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (The attack required no advanced exploits and was achieved by chaining together several common but severe weaknesses within the environment. These included exposed credentials, weak password policies, improper storage of sensitive information, Kerberos misconfigurations, and excessive delegation permissions.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
